{"tool_name":"scmega","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"scmega","home":"https://costalab.github.io/scMEGA","license":"MIT","summary":"Single-cell multi-omic enhancer-based gene regulatory network inference.","addition_date":"2024-01-04T20:30:45.175812Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T14:23:16.759089Z","tool_type":["Library"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:scmega","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"ivan.costa@rwth-aachen.de","name":"Ivan G Costa","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2890-8697","typeEntity":"Person"},{"name":"James S Nagai","typeEntity":"Person"},{"name":"Zhijian Li","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Gene regulatory network analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_1781"},{"term":"Gene regulatory network prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2437"},{"term":"Network visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3925"}]}],"name":"scMEGA","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Pub2Tools","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioadv/vbad003","metadata":{"abstract":"The increasing availability of single-cell multi-omics data allows to quantitatively characterize gene regulation. We here describe scMEGA (Single-cell Multiomic Enhancer-based Gene Regulatory Network Inference) that enables an end-to-end analysis of multi-omics data for gene regulatory network inference including modalities integration, trajectory analysis, enhancer-to-promoter association, network analysis and visualization. This enables to study the complex gene regulation mechanisms for dynamic biological processes, such as cellular differentiation and disease-driven cellular remodeling. We provide a case study on gene regulatory networks controlling myofibroblast activation in human myocardial infarction. Availability and implementation: scMEGA is implemented in R, released under the MIT license and availa